<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 4 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Places I Can Go</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm at the Market.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write simple phrases and sentences on familiar topics. Interactive: Write short role plays to be acted out based on class content.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple phrases and sentences about places like home and school (e.g., "I'm at home."). Can write and perform, based on class content, short role plays about places (e.g., "Where are you?" "I'm at school."). Can write simple captions for classroom images and content (e.g., write "This is the library." for a picture of the library).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0e0e0; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0e0e0; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #e0e0e0; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of familiar places (market, school, home, park, library, hospital, pharmacy). Students identify the places chorally. The teacher then points to a flashcard and asks, "Where am I?" and prompts students to respond with "You are at the [place]." This activates prior vocabulary and prepares for the "I'm at..." structure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher transitions to the writing focus by saying, "Today we will write about places!" The teacher displays a large picture of the market and says, "Look! I'm at the market." The teacher then writes this sentence clearly on the board: "I'm at the market." The teacher highlights 'I'm' and 'at' and models the pronunciation of /m/ in "market" and /sk/ in "school" while writing an example for "school." The teacher explains that 'I'm' is a short way to say 'I am'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Am I writing about a place or an animal?" (Expected answer: A place.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we use 'at' before the place?" (Expected answer: Yes.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'I'm' mean?" (Expected answer: I am.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An illustrated mini-dialogue or short text on a handout titled "Where are you?". The text features two characters, Ana and Pedro, and includes pictures of them at different places (market, library, school).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example Text:</b><br/> Ana: "Hi Pedro! Where are you?"<br/> Pedro: "Hi Ana! I'm at the market. It's busy here. I can buy fruit."<br/> Ana: "Oh, okay. I'm at the library. It's quiet. I can read books."<br/> Pedro: "That's nice! See you later."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing locations: "I'm at school." "I'm at home." Confirming locations: "Are you at school?" "Yes, I'm at school." Using polite expressions like "thank you."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students draw a line from Ana's picture to her location, and from Pedro's picture to his location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Circle the word:</b> Students circle all the 'places' mentioned in the dialogue (e.g., market, library).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students write 'T' or 'F' next to statements: "Pedro is at the library." (F), "Ana is at the library." (T).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple sentences and questions about locations using contractions ("I'm"), prepositions of place ("at," "in," "near"), and present simple tense. Utilizing vocabulary for places, verbs, and adjectives (e.g., home, market, school; buy, read, play; busy, quiet).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of different places and incomplete sentences. They complete the sentences using the correct place word from a word bank and ensure correct use of 'I'm at'.<br/>
                        Example:<br/>
                        1. (Picture of a library) "I'm at the __________."<br/>
                        2. (Picture of a busy market) "I'm at the __________. It's __________ here." (Word bank: market, quiet, busy, library)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sentence Builder (Semi-Controlled Substitution):</b> The teacher provides a list of places (e.g., park, bakery, school) and a list of actions/adjectives (e.g., play, buy bread, study, big, clean). Students write two sentences for each place, using the pattern: "I'm at the [place]. I can [action] here." or "It's [adjective] here."<br/>
                        Example:<br/>
                        - Park: I'm at the park. I can play here.<br/>
                        - Bakery: I'm at the bakery. I can buy bread here.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Question and Answer Writing (Guided Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student chooses three different place flashcards in secret. Then, they write simple questions to ask their partner about their chosen places, and their partner writes the answers. They exchange notebooks to check each other's written responses for correct grammar, spelling, and sentence structure. Students focus on writing "Where are you?" and "I'm at the [place]."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Places I Can Go".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "Our Class Places Storybook"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group chooses one or two places from the vocabulary list (e.g., market, school, gym, cafeteria). They create a page for a class storybook. Each page must feature a drawing or collage of their chosen place. Below the illustration, students must write:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A caption for the place (e.g., "This is the market.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Two simple sentences describing the place or what they can do there (e.g., "I'm at the market. It's busy. I can buy fruit here."). They should use target vocabulary (adjectives like 'busy', verbs like 'buy').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A very short 2-line dialogue between two people meeting at that place, incorporating polite expressions if possible.<br/>
                        Example:<br/>
                        A: "Excuse me, are you at the market?"<br/>
                        B: "Yes, I'm at the market. Thank you for asking!"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, providing support with vocabulary and sentence structure, encouraging correct use of contractions and prepositions of place. The activity directly connects to the theme "I'm at the Market" and scenario "Places I Can Go" by having students write about familiar places and interactions within them.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the group writing task using a simple checklist, and review of the written products (storybook pages).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their completed "Class Places Storybook" page with drawings/collages, captions, sentences, and the short dialogue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist/Rubric):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes at least one caption for the place.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Writes two simple sentences describing the place/action.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correctly uses "I'm at the..." for describing location.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (places, verbs, adjectives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes a short, intelligible 2-line dialogue.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates correct capitalization at sentence start and punctuation (period) at sentence end.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates effectively with group members (observed).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to write about _______." (e.g., places, what I can do).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write 'I'm at the market.' (Check: Yes / No / A little bit)"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and Teacher reflection, and connection to next lesson.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write about today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write about today, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the students achieve the objective of writing simple phrases and sentences about familiar places? Were the contractions "I'm" and prepositions "at" used accurately by most students?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Which vocabulary items or grammatical structures were most challenging for students to write correctly? How can I provide more targeted support for these areas in future lessons?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effective was the group work in fostering collaborative writing? Were the instructions clear enough for the writing task?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments are needed for Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration, especially regarding reinforcing writing accuracy and preparing for the 21st Century Skills Project?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The written storybook pages created today will serve as the foundation for Lesson 5. Students will use their written content to mediate and integrate information, potentially presenting their pages orally or creating a complete class storybook. This provides a tangible product for the next stage of consolidation and project work.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>